Both inputs are already sorted, so there is no need to sort again. Two indexes point to the current value in each array. After choosing the smaller value, the code skips all copies of that value in both arrays before adding it once.

A dry run with `[1, 2, 2]` and `[2, 3]` first adds `1`, then adds `2` and skips the other copies, and finally adds `3`.

The input arrays are not changed. The result list uses extra space.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> unionArray(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2) {
        vector<int> result;
        int i = 0;
        int j = 0;

        while (i < static_cast<int>(nums1.size()) &&
               j < static_cast<int>(nums2.size())) {
            int value;

            if (nums1[i] < nums2[j]) {
                value = nums1[i];
            } else if (nums2[j] < nums1[i]) {
                value = nums2[j];
            } else {
                value = nums1[i];
            }

            result.push_back(value);

            while (i < static_cast<int>(nums1.size()) && nums1[i] == value) {
                ++i;
            }
            while (j < static_cast<int>(nums2.size()) && nums2[j] == value) {
                ++j;
            }
        }

        while (i < static_cast<int>(nums1.size())) {
            int value = nums1[i];
            result.push_back(value);
            while (i < static_cast<int>(nums1.size()) && nums1[i] == value) {
                ++i;
            }
        }

        while (j < static_cast<int>(nums2.size())) {
            int value = nums2[j];
            result.push_back(value);
            while (j < static_cast<int>(nums2.size()) && nums2[j] == value) {
                ++j;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(m + n)` because each input element is passed once.
Space complexity: `O(m + n)` for the returned union.

Here the values are counted in a `Map`, then the map keys are sorted before making the answer. Counting handles duplicates directly, while the final numeric sort keeps the result in the required order.

Dry run: for `[1, 1, 4]` and `[1, 3]`, the map has keys `1`, `3`, and `4`. Sorting those keys gives `[1, 3, 4]`.

This is useful when the input has many repeated values, but the keys still need sorting at the end.

```javascript
class Solution {
    unionArray(nums1, nums2) {
        const counts = new Map();

        for (const value of nums1) {
            counts.set(value, (counts.get(value) || 0) + 1);
        }
        for (const value of nums2) {
            counts.set(value, (counts.get(value) || 0) + 1);
        }

        return Array.from(counts.keys()).sort((a, b) => a - b);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O((m + n) + u log u)`, where `u` is the number of different values.
Space complexity: `O(u)` for the map and returned array.

This uses Java's `TreeSet`. It keeps only one copy of each value and stores the values in sorted order automatically. That makes the solution easy to check, although each insertion costs logarithmic time.

The empty-array cases also work without special handling. If both arrays are empty, the set is empty and the method returns an empty array.

```java
import java.util.TreeSet;

class Solution {
    public int[] unionArray(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {
        TreeSet<Integer> values = new TreeSet<>();

        for (int value : nums1) {
            values.add(value);
        }
        for (int value : nums2) {
            values.add(value);
        }

        int[] result = new int[values.size()];
        int index = 0;
        for (int value : values) {
            result[index++] = value;
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O((m + n) log(m + n))` for the tree insertions.
Space complexity: `O(m + n)` for the tree and result array.

